ehemiah", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "book-of-haggai", "targetName": "Book of Haggai", "context": "Nehemiah's reforms in Jerusalem were influenced by the themes of restoration highlighted in Haggai's prophecies."}], "places": [{"name": "Jerusalem, Israel", "role": "Cultural Center"}, {"name": "Babylon, Iraq", "role": "Exile Origin"}, {"name": "Susa, Iran", "role": "Persian Capital"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Book of Haggai", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "book-of-haggai"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q178338", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Book_of_Haggai"], "tags": ["religious text", "Other Sacred & Wisdom Texts", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Book of Haggai left a notable mark on Classical history, documented across 90 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "The Book of Haggai played a crucial role in revitalizing Jewish identity after the Babylonian Exile, influencing communal worship and identity, which would shape future Jewish religious practices and beliefs.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-15T01:39:32Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#27518972826", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "The Book of Haggai is a book of the Hebrew Bible or Tanakh, and is the third-to-last of the Twelve Minor Prophets. It is a short book, consisting of only two chapters. The historical setting dates around 520 BC, before the Temple had been rebuilt. The original text was written in Biblical Hebrew.", "newValue": "The Book of Haggai, composed around 520 BC, emerges as a pivotal text within the Hebrew Bible, representing the prophetic voice during a critical juncture in Jewish history. Situated in the post-exilic period, it addresses the Jewish community's struggles in rebuilding the Temple in Jerusalem after their return from Babylonian captivity. The prophet Haggai calls for prioritization of the Temple's reconstruction, emphasizing the importance of divine favor and communal restoration, marking a vital moment in Jewish identity and religious practice.

The narrative unfolds against the backdrop of significant socio-political challenges faced by the Jewish populace, who were grappling with economic hardship and demoralization. Haggai's prophetic messages, delivered through a series of exhortations, encouraged the people to resume work on the Temple, emphasizing that their agricultural and economic woes were a result of neglecting God’s house. His call to action led to the eventual rebuilding of the Temple, completed in 516 BC, re-establishing a central place of worship and community identity for the Jewish people.

